Step 0 changes the direction from the prior triage. The paper explicitly says that a nonnegative realizing matrix is not known to be a polynomially checkable certificate in the rational RNIEP, because its entries may need to be irrational (Section 5). A random inverse-generated matrix would also give an unsubstantiated average-case hardness claim.

The paper does provide a cleaner witness family: Lemma 4.1 maps PARTITION instances to spectra \((S/2,S/2,-i_n,\ldots,-i_1)\), and Theorem 5.3 makes a Suleĭmanova–Perfect partition certificate NP-complete over rationals. I’m evaluating that paper-central reduction in its near-critical, high-precision regime, where certificates are exact index partitions and verification is just exact summation.
